<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key><string>en</string>
  <key>CFBundleDisplayName</key><string>ATARU</string>
  <!-- Pre-filled base URL, substituted from ATARU_DEFAULT_BASE_URL in
       Config.xcconfig at build time. Without this entry the xcconfig value
       never reaches the app: AppConfiguration reads this exact key, so the
       "default" silently resolved to an empty string. -->
  <key>ATARUDefaultBaseURL</key><string>$(ATARU_DEFAULT_BASE_URL)</string>
  <!-- The launcher host: TileHealthModel fetches <this>/api/launcher and uses
       it for ONE thing, the online/offline dot on the screens that show a
       machine's state (ServiceCardScreen and RemoteScreen). A DIFFERENT host
       from the assistant base URL, deliberately.

       It has never supplied the destinations themselves - those are the
       native HomeTile enum - so empty, or unreachable, costs the dots and
       nothing else. There is no tile grid any more; the radial launcher
       replaced it. -->
  <key>ATARUTilesURL</key><string>$(ATARU_TILES_URL)</string>
  <key>CFBundleExecutable</key><string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
  <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key><string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
  <key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key><string>6.0</string>
  <key>CFBundleName</key><string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
  <key>CFBundlePackageType</key><string>APPL</string>
  <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key><string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
  <key>CFBundleVersion</key><string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
  <key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key><true/>
  <!--
    Apps ATARU is allowed to ask about. `canOpenURL` returns false for any
    scheme NOT listed here, silently and indistinguishably from the app being
    absent — so a scheme missing from this list makes that app permanently
    invisible to us, and the Media tile would sit forever on its "Swiftfin
    isn't installed" card with Swiftfin installed. Keep in step with
    ExternalApp; `swiftfin` is the URL type Swiftfin registers in its own
    Info.plist.
  -->
  <key>LSApplicationQueriesSchemes</key>
  <array>
    <string>swiftfin</string>
  </array>
  <key>UILaunchScreen</key>
  <dict>
    <key>UIColorName</key><string>LaunchCanvas</string>
  </dict>
  <key>UIUserInterfaceStyle</key><string>Dark</string>
  <key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
  <array>
    <string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
    <string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
    <string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
  </array>
  <key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
  <array>
    <string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
    <string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
    <string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
    <string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
  </array>
  <!--
    Covers both ways in. The old wording promised "only when you tap the
    dictation button", which stopped being true the moment calls existed: a
    call listens hands-free between turns, without anything being held.
  -->
  <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
  <string>ATARU uses the microphone while you hold the dictation button, and between turns during a call, so you can speak a question instead of typing it. It never records at any other time.</string>
  <key>NSSpeechRecognitionUsageDescription</key>
  <string>ATARU transcribes your dictated question on device so it can be sent to your own private assistant.</string>
  <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
  <string>ATARU connects to your own ATARU server on your private network or Tailnet. No data is sent anywhere else.</string>
  <!--
    Lets iOS hand the app an "call ATARU" request from a contact card, the
    Phone app or Siri. Declaring the activity type is what makes the app a
    candidate; actually appearing on the card additionally needs the Siri
    entitlement and at least one donated interaction — see CallIntent.swift.
  -->
  <key>NSUserActivityTypes</key>
  <array>
    <string>INStartCallIntent</string>
    <!-- Deprecated predecessor, still emitted on some paths. iOS only delivers
         activity types an app has claimed here, so an unclaimed one arrives
         nowhere — indistinguishable from a handler that does not work. -->
    <string>INStartAudioCallIntent</string>
  </array>
  <!-- NO NSContactsUsageDescription, deliberately. Adding ATARU to Contacts
       hands iOS a vCard through the share sheet and the SYSTEM writes the
       entry; this app never constructs a CNContactStore, so it must never
       claim a reason to read the address book. A usage string left behind
       here would be a promise about access the app does not have and does not
       want. See Core/Telephony/ATARUContact.swift. -->
  <!-- NO NSFaceIDUsageDescription either, and for the same reason. There was
       one - "ATARU can require Face ID before showing your private records" -
       backed by a `BiometricLock` class that nothing in the app ever
       constructed: no screen offered the toggle, no view asked whether it was
       unlocked. So the string promised a protection the app did not have, on
       the one screen (the privacy sheet in Settings) where iOS shows a user
       what an app claims. Both are gone. Re-add the string with the feature,
       never before it. -->
  <key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key><false/>

  <!--
    `voip` keeps the call's audio session running when the app is backgrounded
    or the screen locks, which a call obviously has to survive.

    It is also the mode PushKit requires, and that is the piece this demo does
    not have: incoming calls here are reported locally by the app while it runs.
    A real "ATARU rings you" needs a VoIP push from the server, and note the
    contract that comes with it — from iOS 13, an app that receives a VoIP push
    and does NOT call reportNewIncomingCall gets terminated, and repeat
    offenders lose push privileges. Do not add PushKit without wiring the
    report unconditionally.
  -->
  <key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
  <array>
    <string>audio</string>
    <string>voip</string>
  </array>

  <!--
    App Transport Security.

    ATARU talks to a server on the user's own Tailnet, which is reached over a
    100.64.0.0/10 address (RFC 6598, the CGNAT range Tailscale uses). Apple's
    NSAllowsLocalNetworking exception covers RFC 1918 and .local but NOT that
    range, so it alone is not enough — hence NSAllowsArbitraryLoads.

    NSAllowsLocalNetworking must NOT be added back "for good measure". On
    iOS 10 and later, the presence of that key (or the WebContent/Media
    variants) causes NSAllowsArbitraryLoads to be IGNORED — the granular keys
    exist so an app can offer a broad exception to old OS versions and a narrow
    one to new. Setting both silently reinstates the narrow policy and every
    Tailnet request fails with NSURLErrorAppTransportSecurityRequiresSecureConnection
    (-1022).

    What actually restricts insecure connections is the app, not ATS:
    AppConfiguration.validate rejects every plain-http URL in a Release build,
    and in Debug accepts one only for a private host (RFC 1918, 100.64/10,
    .local, .ts.net). ATS is the permissive layer here and the app is the
    strict one, which is the opposite of the usual arrangement and is
    deliberate — see PRIVACY.md.

    The better long-term answer is to stop using plain http at all: run
    `tailscale serve` on the host and connect to the https://…ts.net name with
    its real certificate. Then none of this applies.
  -->
  <key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
  <dict>
    <key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoads</key><true/>
  </dict>
</dict>
</plist>
